A||||6||Richard Parnell||ATF Saga Part IV||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||04-28-2000||02:23 PM||||As a lot of you know there has been the ongoing saga of what fluid works best in the clutch hydraulic system.  For some ATF works fine, others use different weight oils, while for some the standard DOT fluid works as it should.  In my case I went to the ATF out of desperation and it worked, for a while.  First I noticed that the rubber sealer under the resevoir cap melted.  Just recently the car has begun to shift hard again, so last night I checked everything out.  Did the gravity feed technique (which by the way works better than any other method I found for getting air out).  It still shifted hard so I was checking that the clutch pedal and all washers and shims were ok .  Well I found a leak coming from the master cylinder plunger inside the the car where the plunger comes through.  Now my question.  I have the new slave and master cylinders and am going to replace them.  But what should I flush the line with? I want to be sure and not use something that will damage the seals in the new cylinders.  Anyone?||||reg||5
Z||000001||lennyb4||04-28-2000||02:27 PM||||I would use DOT 4 fluid (yes i said 4). It has a higher heat resistance and wont damage seals like ATF of PS fluid will. I would also get a quart or two extra and flush the system real good.||||reg||7
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||04-28-2000||02:48 PM||||Thanks Lennb4-  I am planning on using the DOT 4.  I was just wondering if I need to use something to  "cut" the ATF, like a solvent, but that is still safe for the cylinder seals.  I plan on flushing the line before installing the cylinders.||||reg||7
Z||000003||mwbackus||04-28-2000||03:35 PM||||Wash with alcohol (methanol), and blow everything "completely dry" with compressed air before adding the new hydraulic fluid.  Not only will methanol function as a solvent for both ATF and old brake fluids, but it will also remove any moisture present due its azeotropic and anhydrous properties.<BR>As I have stated before, using ATF in the hydraulic clutch system will inevitably result in a clutch failure.  It is NOT compatible with the seals used in these systems.<BR>If you would have listened to the objections I posted during your original discussion of this subject back in December;<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001667.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001667.html</A> <P>you would not have wasted all this time and money. :Þ<P>Mike B.<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 04-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||04-28-2000||04:07 PM||||MWBACKUS- You're right, I'm an idiot.  I just don't listen sometimes and end up bitin' the big one in the end.  I just hope that the master and slave I bought are good.  I've had the complication before of not only trying to fix a problem but having the added mix of a faulty (new) part thrown in.  Well, tomorrow morning it's out with the ATF and old master and slave and in with new parts and DOT 4, I'm determined to get this problem solved long term.  My wife thinks I'm nuts but I'm sure that's a common opinion amongst Fiero owner's friends and spouses.||||reg||9
Z||000005||fiero56||04-28-2000||05:26 PM||||I used the ATF, the seals expanded in both brand new master and slave cylinders(possible this doesn't happen on older seals?), and I ended up redoing the system. I used compressed air to get all the old ATF out, and lots of DOT3 to make sure it was clear. I have since put about 12,000 trouble-free miles. My recommendation would be to stick to factory specs(I am not ignoring those specs anymore). DOT4 *should* work, but I am not taking anymore chances. <P>Side note: if you have a cast iron slave sylinder, take it easy on torquing the bleeder.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Frank R||04-28-2000||07:23 PM|||| MWBackus, your previous info and your advice is very good.  I'm an old guy with lots of experience.  Any system designed for brake fluid must continue to use the fluid designed for same.  Many systems use different fluids.  Old British and European cars used a fluid called Girling fluid.  Any other fluid would contaminate the system.  I personally use the synthetic brake fluid for many reasons.  The best reason for using synthetic fluid is it never needs to be changed, and very importantly......the fluid does NOT absorb any moisture.  The boiling point is also much higher.  Any arbitrary changing of automotive fluids is very bad for the test car.  If an error is made, the seals must all be changed before correct fluids are installed....<BR>Frank R...||||reg||6
